1
00:00:00,134 --> 00:00:06,021
Two products. Almost identical. One costs twice as much, because of a name.

2
00:00:06,727 --> 00:00:11,205
Why do we pay for that? To answer it, we have to go back more than four thousand years.

3
00:00:12,663 --> 00:00:18,143
This is the story of the brand: where it started, how it changed, and what AI is changing now.

4
00:00:21,531 --> 00:00:26,051
The word brand comes from an Old English word for fire, or a piece of burning wood.

5
00:00:27,583 --> 00:00:47,069
More than four thousand years ago, people in the Indus Valley carved small stone seals, likely to show who goods belonged to. Paintings in ancient Egyptian tombs show cattle being branded. And Roman brickmakers and potters pressed their names into clay, while wine jars in Pompeii carried the producer's name.

6
00:00:48,083 --> 00:00:51,556
The first job of a brand was simple. Say who this belongs to, and who made it.

7
00:00:54,443 --> 00:01:00,961
Then the mark took on a second job. In 1266, England made bakers' marks compulsory on bread.

8
00:01:01,484 --> 00:01:07,724
If a loaf was short weight, you could find the baker. A brand became a promise you could hold someone to.

9
00:01:09,488 --> 00:01:16,728
The industrial age broke the link between maker and buyer. Goods travelled hundreds of miles, and the person who made them was a stranger.

10
00:01:18,352 --> 00:01:29,363
So the mark had to carry the trust instead. When Britain opened its trade mark register on the first of January 1876, Bass Brewery's red triangle became trade mark number one.

11
00:01:30,479 --> 00:01:36,999
Legend says an employee queued all night to be first. There's no record that it happened. The trade mark is real.

12
00:01:38,745 --> 00:01:47,636
By the 1880s, manufacturers were giving their brands personalities, so shoppers asked for the brand instead of taking the shopkeeper's word.

13
00:01:48,099 --> 00:02:01,069
In May 1931, Neil McElroy at Procter and Gamble wrote a memo proposing one manager and one team for each brand, run like its own business. That memo is where modern brand management starts.

14
00:02:02,025 --> 00:02:10,905
Then science caught up with what marketers suspected. In a 2004 study, volunteers tasted Coke and Pepsi, partly inside a brain scanner.

15
00:02:11,325 --> 00:02:21,445
When they were shown the Coke can before a sip, it changed which drink they preferred, and lit up brain areas linked to memory. The name was changing how people judged the taste.

16
00:02:23,297 --> 00:02:34,781
Brands also tell other people, and ourselves, who we are. Back in 1899, the economist Thorstein Veblen called it conspicuous consumption: buying things partly to show your status.

17
00:02:35,797 --> 00:02:40,259
The internet flipped the direction. Brands used to broadcast. Now customers answer back, in public.

18
00:02:41,669 --> 00:02:54,627
Glossier launched in 2014 out of a beauty blog, Into the Gloss, which asked its readers what products they wished existed. The brand was no longer only what the company said. It was what people said back.

19
00:02:56,129 --> 00:03:14,489
Now there's a new reader. In a July 2026 survey of more than two thousand US adults, about three in ten said they use AI chatbots to research products. Among people who use AI, about six in ten had found a new brand through a chatbot's recommendation.

20
00:03:15,429 --> 00:03:22,509
And more of what brands say is now drafted by AI. A team of three can publish like a department.

21
00:03:23,497 --> 00:03:30,897
Which makes the old problem harder, not easier. When anyone can make content, the brands that stand out are the ones that sound like themselves every time.

22
00:03:33,197 --> 00:03:44,615
Look at the whole timeline, and the mark keeps changing. A seal. A burn. A stamp on a loaf. A trade mark. A feed. An answer from an AI.

23
00:03:45,297 --> 00:03:57,621
But the job never changed. A brand exists for two reasons. Choosing is risky, so a brand tells you what to expect. And people want to belong, so a brand tells the world something about who chose it.

24
00:03:59,392 --> 00:04:04,112
That's the first principle. A brand is a promise, made distinctive, and kept consistently.

25
00:04:05,404 --> 00:04:11,884
That's what this channel is for: one idea in brand marketing at a time, taken back to first principles.

26
00:04:12,952 --> 00:04:18,432
And at Mavic, we're building AI that works from your brand, so what you make with it still sounds like you.

27
00:04:20,220 --> 00:04:25,459
Keeping a brand's promise is getting harder. We're building the tools to help you keep yours. Follow along.
